{
  "assessment": "The change replaces Python's built-in `round()` with a manual remainder-based calculation that incorrectly rounds remainders equal to exactly half the step size (2 out of 5) upwards instead of downwards. This introduces a bias towards higher billing steps, directly causing the exact discrepancy described in the task where a 2.0 kg quote becomes a 2.5 kg bill.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "actual": "The manual remainder logic uses `>= 2` as its threshold for rounding up on a step of 5. Standard nearest-neighbor rounding dictates that remainders less than half the step (i.e., < 2.5) must round down. This causes any weight with a remainder of exactly 2 to be rounded to the upper step instead of the current one.",
      "at": "shiprate/weights.py",
      "call": "python -c \"from shiprate.weights import billable; print(billable(102))\"",
      "defect": "Incorrect rounding threshold for integer remainders. When STEP_DG is 5, a remainder of 2 falls below the mathematical midpoint (2.5) and should round down to the nearest step, but the new logic forces it up.",
      "expected": "100"
    }
  ]
}
